{"title":"0.999...","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Basic_Idea\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Basic Idea</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Proofs\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proofs</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Fractions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Fractions</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Algebraic_Manipulation\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Algebraic Manipulation</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Infinite_series\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Infinite series</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Limits\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Limits</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Basic_Idea\">Basic Idea</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=0.999...&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Basic Idea\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/f/1ef8a53c4083457f37ce1010c0bd14b163d00f67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.999\\ldots$\" width=\"64\" height=\"12\" /> (or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/b/c7bc4a36780b81a1727c9cb1f5df2e5a1098f517.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.\\overline{9}$\" width=\"23\" height=\"15\" />) is an equivalent representation of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Integer\" title=\"Integer\">integer</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.  \n</p><p>It is intuitively clear what the dots after the nines mean. However, to resolve the problem, one needs mathematics beyond the elementary school level math that is needed to understand the question.\n</p><p>What do the dots after the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />s actually mean?\n</p><p>One potential definition is to say that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/a/a1a0e9f766104626083e80516be6a885a48c5adf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$.999...$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"49\" height=\"14\" /> is the limit of the sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/5/a65f0ea017dc0e1238aa2c3ebbfd17b617ff5f7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$.9,.99,.999,...$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"111\" height=\"17\" />. That is to say, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"13\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/1/f614ddae105e7ef7c1d690c99b71f334de33b5a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$.999...9$\" width=\"55\" height=\"12\" /> where there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> nines. Then we can say that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/5/0257135e7acb721b3aee6426a6b83afec820bb43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$.999....$\" width=\"50\" height=\"12\" /> does in fact equal <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. \n</p><p>But in some senses, this is not the most satisfying definition. When we say that the limit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"13\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> goes to infinity is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, that means that for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/4/ca47de052eb3fb5454694d9bce3561ebff831a1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\epsilon&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" />, there exists an integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/5/0755192f1ed3b77317e8d60ec40edb5bad5d7a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/f/d2f13fc60b46d8b8a3144377c789c1b8afa8ee41.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|a_n-L|&lt;\\epsilon$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"94\" height=\"18\" /> for all integers greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />. So in fact, we had to resort to limit definitions to resolve this problem. If you think about it, there is no <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/5/965a91ea46832772bbc200fe53dd6c0d102def67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"17\" />. \n</p><p>Another mathematical tidbit: how do we know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/a/a1a0e9f766104626083e80516be6a885a48c5adf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$.999...$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"49\" height=\"14\" /> is a real number? Intuitively, a real number line seems to be continuous, so such a limit seems to be a real number. But how do we know that there are no 'gaps' so it could possibly not be a real number. But lets prove it.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/3/e73a6a95a4f41b764cccfa536ddb261807d935b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S=\\{a_n|n\\in\\mathbb{Z}\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"122\" height=\"18\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/9/779b9594ee1f3b156dfe6ec70e1d4a386fcb454e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1\\in S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"15\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is nonempty. It is easy to see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/1/b21b52240e97251d53c55392e15dd4c722eb85f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n=1-(.1)^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"110\" height=\"18\" /> since it is a finite geometric series. But then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/3/2735bfce5c668704989adcbe2ca30e00afa858f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-(.1)^n&lt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"101\" height=\"18\" />. Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> has an upper bound of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. But by the least upper bound axiom of real numbers, if a set of real numbers has a upper bound, then it has a least upper bound (that is a real number). \n</p><p>Suppose that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> had a least upper bound, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/f/3fffc14c948a05ca9822f1af77eb4c812530b655.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L&#39;$\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/f/42f772105a1ea1532e13152642c3fa13b56702a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L&#39;&lt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"49\" height=\"14\" />. But then we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/2/b6207fbca68bb363a756da716add7d591e043eb1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-(.1)^n\\le L&#39;&lt;1\\implies 0&lt; 1-L\\le (.1)^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"330\" height=\"18\" />. But that is nonsense since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/d/24de48c86e4040ada6661dafd0de80b513fa3a34.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log_{.1} (1-L)\\ge n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"127\" height=\"18\" />, which would be a least upper bound for the natural numbers. Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, the limit of the sequence, is a least upper bound. Hence that expression is in fact a real number.\n</p><p>The proofs below show how to evaluate the limit, but they implicitly use the definition given above. \n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proofs\">Proofs</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=0.999...&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Proofs\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Fractions\">Fractions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=0.999...&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Fractions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/d/9cd48c056ca8d131a24b9e45441e2a20d6b11041.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac 13 = 0.\\overline{3} = 0.333\\ldots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"149\" height=\"37\" />, multiplying both sides by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/f/b1fade827854b9cb915c2821c1e89442b3a64d40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 = 0.999\\ldots$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"97\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>Alternatively, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/c/3ac2fb0bfdfdc2fe4851f77bc9a78b88cd3af3f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac 19 = 0.\\overline{1} = 0.111\\ldots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"149\" height=\"37\" />, and then multiply both sides by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. \n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Algebraic_Manipulation\">Algebraic Manipulation</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=0.999...&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Algebraic Manipulation\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/2/7a2ab5ad42336da9fd35a4701c9e849782d4f9d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 0.999\\ldots$\" width=\"98\" height=\"12\" /> Then we have\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/4/b74469de0d8a3abaa5f4612b8d44ee5ad68a205d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10x = 9.999\\ldots$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"117\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/3/233ba224193ee42ec14da2a1b2c1841f94f63284.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phantom{10}x = 0.999\\ldots$\" width=\"117\" height=\"12\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>Subtracting, \n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/7/cc77e7d69a4bd11f3b8a11ae43cae6f97a452624.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9x = 9$\" width=\"52\" height=\"12\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/b/19b74e00d5f1db1e30c3aa35feff487bebfcbfd8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phantom{9}x = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"51\" height=\"12\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Infinite_series\">Infinite series</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=0.999...&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Infinite series\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/7/c0790728328bd92ec568c11fd893412ba7ee7aba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.999\\ldots = 0.9 + 0.09 + 0.009 + \\ldots = \\frac{9}{10} + \\frac{9}{100} + \\frac{9}{1000} + \\ldots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"479\" height=\"38\" /></center>\n<p>This is an <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinite_geometric_series\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Infinite geometric series\">infinite geometric series</a>, so\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/2/ae2f54a6432e25455253212354eaa9db3238648c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.999\\ldots = \\frac{\\frac{9}{10}}{1 - \\frac{1}{10}} = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"178\" height=\"49\" /></center>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Limits\">Limits</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=0.999...&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Limits\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/0/620a2bd6126eb620424ff784f8362470022fb71c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.999\\ldots = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}0.\\underbrace{ 99\\ldots9 }_{n} = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k = 1}^n\\frac{9}{10^k} = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(1-\\frac{1}{10^n}\\right) = 1-\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{10^n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"593\" height=\"49\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/9196e45a2d68e3f48c4f60237064f77b19ddb11e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=0.999...&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Geometric_sequence\" title=\"Geometric sequence\">Geometric sequence</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017155532\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.035 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.107 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 263/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 8753/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:6785:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017155532 and revision id 188621. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}